## Why This Is Asked
This question probes end-to-end deployment of multilingual fact-checking with RAG, a practical need for large product support teams. It blends privacy, latency, and explainability in production.

## Key Concepts
- Knowledge graphs and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)
- Multilingual encoders and cross-lingual alignment
- Citations, confidence scoring, audit logging
- KB versioning, canaries, rollback, drift monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code illustrating the RAG flow
async function veracityPipeline(query, lang) {
  const claim = extractClaim(query);
  const facts = await retrieveFacts(claim, lang); // KG + external sources
  const resp = composeAnswer(claim, facts, lang);
  return { resp, citations: facts.map(f => f.source), confidence: aggregateConfidence(facts) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle conflicting sources or low-confidence claims?
- How would you evaluate latency vs. accuracy during rollout?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests on-device multilingual NLP for real-time support escalation, balancing simplicity with practical constraints.

## Key Concepts
- **On-device inference**: Use TensorFlow Lite or ONNX Runtime Mobile for sub-100ms inference
- **Multilingual code-switch handling**: Character n-grams are language-agnostic; use Unicode normalization for emoji
- **Hybrid rule+ML approach**: Rules catch obvious cases (fast path), ML handles ambiguity
- **Latency targets**: 120ms p99 includes preprocessing + inference + postprocessing
- **Privacy**: On-device only, no data transmission, local differential privacy for any updates
- **Evaluation**: Offline metrics followed by staged rollout with careful monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// On-device scoring with TF-Lite
function scoreEscalation(messages, tfliteInterpreter) {
  const input = preprocessMessages(messages); // normalize, tokenize
  tfliteInterpreter.setInput(input);
  tfliteInterpreter.invoke();
  const output = tfliteInterpreter.output(0);
  return { score: output[0], threshold: 0.7 }; // escalate if above threshold
}

function checkRules(messages) {
  const urgentPatterns = [/\bhelp\b/i, /\burgent\b/i, /speak.*human/i];
  const urgentEmojis = ['🚨', '🙋', '😡', '😭'];
  const text = messages.join(' ');
  if (urgentPatterns.some(p => p.test(text)) || urgentEmojis.some(e => text.includes(e))) {
    return { escalate: true, confidence: 0.95, source: 'rule' };
  }
  return null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to drift or new escalation cues?
  - Use online learning with local batch updates, or periodic model refresh via federated learning
- How to measure impact on support workload and false positives?
  - Track: tickets deflected, false positive rate, escalation accuracy, user satisfaction scores

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates practical on-device ML expertise, cross-language support, privacy-preserving inference, and the ability to balance model size with functionality in a realistic chat application.

## Key Concepts
- On-device machine learning and privacy-first design
- Lightweight multilingual models for resource-constrained environments
- Explainability through keyword attribution and contribution scoring
- Offline labeling pipelines and evaluation methodologies

## Code Example
```javascript
function predict(text) {
  const keywords = extractKeywords(text);
  const embeddings = model.embed(keywords);
  const intent = classifier.predict(embeddings);
  const tone = sentimentAnalyzer.predict(embeddings);
  const explanations = explainer.attribute(keywords, intent);
  return { intent, tone, explanations };
}
```

This design meets all constraints: under 15 MB, on-device execution, multilingual support (EN/ES/PT), explainable predictions, and strong privacy controls.

